WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
One study says it works like magic. Do you believe it?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
A finding begins the work of checking.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
A 2015 collaboration replicated psychology studies.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
It examined a selected set of published studies.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
The results emphasized methods and accumulating evidence.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Ask how it was done, who took part and how it was analyzed.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Document the process for comparison and repetition.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Compare findings alongside their conditions.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
The bridge is a metaphor for repeated checking.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
A failed replication does not automatically imply fraud.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Success does not guarantee every context.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Differences may revise an explanation or its scope.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Look beyond one paper to later and combined evidence.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Look for preregistration, materials and transparent methods.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Check how uncertainty is explained.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Reliability need not mean never changing.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
It includes letting new evidence change understanding.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Let curiosity stand up to further questions.
